WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:03.810
- [Narrator] The dollar is smiling.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:07.740
Not literally, but its value
has reached its highest point

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.110
since the early 2000s.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:13.800
The increase fits into a theory
known as the dollar smile.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.930
It says the dollar typically gets stronger

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.790
when the U.S. economy is healthy.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:21.210
Like when it saw a period
of economic growth in 2014

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.810
or when the global economy
faces unexpected shocks,

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.030
like we're seeing in 2022.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.280
But it loses value when the U.S. economy

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.220
is on a downward trend compared
to the rest of the world.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.190
Like in 2008 during the Great Recession.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.960
That's how the dollar smiles.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.760
- So the dollar reaching
multidecade highs.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.080
And that's because we have the Fed

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.910
that's in the middle of hiking rates.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.500
We also have people that are very worried

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.580
about the state of economy.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.460
I mean, normally this kind of
increase comes like over time,

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.540
over two, three years.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.910
So this is an unexpected surge hire.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.380
- [Narrator] While a
strong dollar might seem

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.970
like something to be happy about,

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:57.450
it's impact across the world
can be more complicated.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.970
Here's how the dollar starts to smile

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.370
and the unexpected
consequences it can have

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:03.933
for the global economy.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.470
Let's look back at that smile.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.990
One of the main reasons the
dollar remains attractive

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.960
to investors at both ends
of the economic spectrum

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.720
is because it is a dominant
global reserve currency,

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.700
which means every country
keeps a certain amount

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.040
of U.S. dollars in their central banks.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.290
- For foreign economies,
the wide use of the dollar

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.020
allows borrowers to have
access to a broad pool

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.090
of lenders and investors.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.370
- [Narrator] According to the
international monetary fund,

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:34.370
nearly 7 trillion U.S. dollars
are held by 149 countries.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.690
- Investors like the
dollar because it's stable.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.010
They know that you have a country

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.780
where there's a central
bank that is stepped in

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.240
to shore up markets and
intervenes in the economy

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.790
at whatever cost to make
sure it doesn't crash.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.010
- [Narrator] Because so many
countries rely on the dollar

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.650
for trade and various transactions,

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.600
that means there's always demand for it,

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.280
which makes it more valuable.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.710
But that demand fluctuates
and depends largely

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.990
on the state of the U.S. economy.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.640
When the economy is healthy,

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.010
investors bet on economic
growth that will lead

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.170
to eventual interest rate increases.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.870
The theory dubs this end greed mode.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.720
- If you're buying currencies,
you wanna hold the one

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.730
that's gonna give you
a higher rate of return

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.920
that's linked to your central bank's rate.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.510
- [Narrator] On the
other end of the smile,

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.160
when the economy is struggling,

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.670
investors can flee to the
dollar as a more secure asset.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.640
That's fear mode, the end we're at now.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.920
- So the dollar's benefited from basically

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.740
a confluence of events,
volatile financial markets,

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.050
fears around growth in the global economy,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.000
supply chain issues stemming from the fact

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.310
that China is still dealing with COVID,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.450
and we have a war in Europe

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.550
that's spreading an energy crisis there,

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.050
and we have global inflation.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.510
So all of that combined has
people basically jumping

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.040
into the U.S. dollar as a haven.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.110
- [Narrator] A haven is
an asset that can retain

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:49.770
or even increase its value
during an economic downturn.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.320
The dollar's strength is
determined by measuring it

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.240
against other global currencies.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.180
When it gets stronger,
other currencies grow weaker

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.770
by comparison.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.710
In July, the dollar
and Euro reached parity

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.200
for the first time in 20 years.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.870
And as the value of other currencies fall,

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.060
the impact of the strong dollar ripples

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.410
through the global economy.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:15.510
The dollar is the main
currency used in global trade.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.940
That means that things
like food and energy

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.190
are usually priced in
dollars when they're bought

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.260
and sold among certain countries,

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.480
even if they aren't buying from the U.S.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.190
- So basically their currency's weakened,

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.500
which makes everything
more expensive for them,

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.200
which means price increases,
which is inflation.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.850
So you're basically, there's this concept

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.130
that a stronger dollar exports inflation

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.780
to emerging market countries.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.460
- [Narrator] But a strong
dollar can actually have

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.440
the opposite effect for Americans

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.480
by helping bring prices down.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:43.830
- Strong dollar benefits you

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.830
because it makes importing
goods and services cheaper.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:47.663
People like that.

00:03:47.663 --> 00:03:49.920
It makes traveling abroad
for Americans cheaper.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.510
That's also a good thing.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.910
- [Narrator] But as imports
get cheaper for the U.S.,

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.970
exports get more expensive
for other countries.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.040
U.S. products become less affordable

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.260
and therefore less attractive to consumers

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.700
and businesses abroad.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.140
That means that U.S. companies

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:07.200
that make a significant portion
of their profits overseas

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.270
can see a decline in sales.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.970
Companies have already
taken a hit this year.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.390
Microsoft, Salesforce and
Coca-Cola are just a few

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:17.130
that have pointed out the negative impact

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.623
of the strong dollar on
their financial results.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.770
Here's Microsoft's
chief marketing officer,

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.780
after the company filed an 8-K,

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.030
which is a form that
reports unexpected changes

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:28.020
to shareholders.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.010
- Say, you know, if you saw
the 8-K today it was purely,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.293
it was purely currency.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.020
Continued strength of the dollar,

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.330
essentially is what we
talked about in that filing.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.780
- [Narrator] About 40% of
S&amp;P 500 companies' revenue

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:44.850
comes from outside the U.S.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.610
And a decline in sales and future earnings

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.590
tends to make stocks fall.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.440
But these lower earnings
and reduced import costs

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.980
can also help curve inflation
by bringing prices down

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:58.590
and forcing companies to slow spending.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.060
- You're cooling things down when you have

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.070
a stronger currency, very indirectly.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:05.070
So some analysts will say,
it's almost the equivalent

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.270
of a rate hike.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:07.410
So it's kind of tricky.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.810
It's good, but when it gets too strong,

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.580
it can start being damaging.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.560
- [Narrator] Experts expect
the dollar to stabilize

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.980
later this year, but it's
unclear when that might be.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.840
- What you would need for
the dollar to stabilize

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.790
is inflation data to cool.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.190
You would need geopolitical
uncertainty to fade,

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:26.130
and you'd need a greater
belief that the global economy

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:29.340
is back on track, and that
growth is going to return,

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.370
and that financial markets
are going to calm down.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.530
- [Narrator] When the global
economy starts to settle

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:37.133
the dollar will likely stop smiling.

00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:39.883
(pleasant music)

